POST = dict(
 kicker="Websites and Google",
 card="Why Google suspends trade profiles, how to find the real reason, the evidence that helps, an appeal you can copy and how to stop it happening again.",
 title="Google Business Profile Suspended? How to Fix It",
 desc="Google Business Profile suspended? Find the reason, fix the profile, gather the right evidence and appeal once, properly. Steps and an appeal text for 2026.",
 h1="Google Business Profile suspended? *Here is how to get it back.*",
 lede="If your Google Business Profile is suspended, do not make a new one. Open Google's appeals tool to see which policy was flagged, fix the profile so it follows the guidelines, gather documents that show the same business name and address, then submit one careful appeal. Google says appeals can only be submitted once and decisions can take up to 5 business days, so getting it right first time matters more than getting it in fast.",
 ticks=["Why trade profiles get suspended", "The exact appeal steps and the evidence that helps", "An appeal text to copy and a checklist to stay safe"],
 html="""<h2 id="short-answer">What should you do when your Google Business Profile is suspended?</h2><p>Stop, breathe and do these five things in order:</p><ol><li><strong>Do not create a new profile.</strong> Google's help pages say this plainly. A second profile for the same business is itself against the rules and makes your case harder.</li><li><strong>Open the appeals tool</strong> while signed in to the Google account that owns the profile. It shows the date of the decision, the reason, a link to the policy you broke and whether you can appeal.</li><li><strong>Fix the profile first.</strong> Read the policy it links to and change whatever does not follow it, such as the business name, the address or the service area.</li><li><strong>Gather evidence</strong> where the business name and address match the profile exactly.</li><li><strong>Submit one appeal</strong> with the evidence attached and a short, calm explanation. Then wait for the email.</li></ol><p>That is the whole process. The rest of this guide helps you find the real cause, choose the right documents and write the appeal, because Google notes that an appeal can only be submitted once.</p>

<h2 id="why-suspended">Why do contractor profiles get suspended?</h2><p>Google suspends profiles that do not follow its <a href="https://support.google.com/business/answer/3038177">guidelines for representing your business</a>. For trades, the same few problems come up again and again:</p><ul><li><strong>Keywords in the business name.</strong> Google says your name should match your real world name, as used on your signage, website and stationery, and that adding unnecessary information "could result in the suspension of your Business Profile". "Smith Plumbing" is fine if that is your registered name. "Smith Plumbing Emergency Plumber Leeds" is not.</li><li><strong>Showing a home address.</strong> If you travel to customers, you are a service area business. Google's own example is a plumber working from home, who should clear the address from the profile.</li><li><strong>An address you do not work from.</strong> P.O. boxes, remote mailboxes and virtual offices are not eligible. A desk at a coworking space only counts if it has clear signage and is staffed during business hours.</li><li><strong>More than one profile.</strong> Google allows one profile per business per location. Extra profiles for nearby towns, or a profile at an employee's house to look like a second branch, are a common cause.</li><li><strong>A phone number or website you do not control.</strong> Numbers and links must go straight to your business, not to a lead service or a page that sends people elsewhere.</li><li><strong>A huge service area.</strong> Whitespark's suspension guide, updated in 2026, lists a service area stretching more than about two hours' drive from your base as a risk.</li><li><strong>Big edits all at once.</strong> Trade press has reported suspensions after a name, address or category change, including changes pushed automatically by listing tools. Several edits in one sitting can trigger a review.</li></ul><p>Sometimes nothing is wrong at all. Trade press describes categories such as plumbing and HVAC as high risk for Google, because they attract a lot of fake listings, so honest businesses get caught in the checks. That is frustrating, but the fix is the same: prove you are real.</p>

<h2 id="suspended-or-disabled">Suspended, disabled or account restricted: what is the difference?</h2><p>The word on your screen tells you where to start:</p><table><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What it means</th><th>Where to appeal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Profile suspended</td><td>The profile broke a guideline and is hidden from the public</td><td>Google Business Profile appeals tool</td></tr><tr><td>Profile disabled</td><td>The profile has been switched off over a policy problem</td><td>Same appeals tool; the tool shows whether it can be appealed</td></tr><tr><td>Account restricted</td><td>Your Google account was flagged, so every profile you manage is suspended and you cannot create or claim new ones</td><td>First the restrictions page in your Google account, then each profile in the appeals tool once the account is cleared</td></tr><tr><td>Manager removed</td><td>A manager's Google account was suspended; the profile itself is fine</td><td>That manager's own Google account</td></tr></tbody></table><p>One detail matters for small firms: if the <strong>owner's</strong> Google account is removed, Google removes every location that account owns. Keep the business profile on a business Google account that you control, not on a former employee's or an agency's personal login.</p>

<h2 id="evidence">What evidence helps a Google Business Profile appeal?</h2><p>Google's help page lists the documents that can strengthen an appeal:</p><ul><li>Official business registration</li><li>A business licence (license in the USA)</li><li>Tax certificates</li><li>Utility bills for the business, such as electricity, phone, water or internet</li></ul><p>The detail that matters most is simple: <strong>the business name and address on the documents must match the profile.</strong> If your profile says "Kerr Electrical Ltd" and your bill says "J Kerr", fix one or the other before you appeal, or explain the link with a registration document that shows both.</p><p>Trades working from home often worry about using a home bill. That is normal for a service area business. The address is hidden from customers on the profile, but Google can still see it, and it needs to match. Trade press advice also suggests photos of permanent signage and your premises. If you work from a van, a clear photo of it signwritten with your name and phone number does the same job. Use clear photos taken this month, and keep people's faces out of them.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-appeal">How do you appeal a suspended Google Business Profile, step by step?</h2><ol><li>Fix the profile so it follows the guidelines. Make the changes before you appeal, not after.</li><li>Open the <a href="https://support.google.com/business/?p=manage_appeals">Google Business Profile appeals tool</a> and confirm the right Google account.</li><li>Select the suspended profile, then the decision you want to appeal.</li><li>Read the reason and the linked policy. Make sure your fix answers it.</li><li>Attach every document at once. You can add several files.</li><li>Write a short explanation in the text box (template below).</li><li>Tick the box confirming all evidence is attached and submit.</li><li>Leave the profile alone while you wait. Do not keep editing or send a second appeal for the same issue.</li></ol><p>Google says appeal reviews and decisions can take up to 5 business days. Owners and consultants report waits from a few days to several weeks, so plan for the slower end. If the appeal is denied, Google offers a form to request an additional review, where you can add evidence you did not send the first time. If your business is in the EEA, Google also points to extra options under the Digital Services Act.</p>

<h2 id="appeal-text">What should you write in the appeal?</h2><p>Keep it short, factual and polite. Google reviewers read a lot of appeals, and anger or long stories do not help. Copy this and change the details:</p><p>"Hello. Kerr Electrical Ltd is a registered electrical contractor based in [town]. We travel to customers' homes and businesses, so the address on our profile is hidden and our service area covers [towns or postcodes] within about [time] of our base. After reading the policy linked in this decision, we have [changed the business name to match our registration / removed the duplicate profile / hidden our home address]. Attached are our company registration and a recent utility bill, both showing the same name and address as the profile, plus a photo of our signwritten van. We are a real, local business and would be grateful if you could review the profile again. Thank you."</p><p>If you genuinely do not know what went wrong, say what you checked: "We have checked the name, address, phone number, website and categories against the guidelines and they match our registration documents."</p>

<h2 id="while-you-wait">How do you keep getting calls while you wait?</h2><p>A suspended profile disappears from Google Maps, and for many trades that is where most calls come from. Use the wait to protect your work:</p><ul><li><strong>Text your recent customers</strong> and past customers to remind them you are still trading. Something like: "Hi Sam, Jake from Kerr Electrical here. Our Google listing is down for a few days, so if you or anyone you know needs us, call or text this number directly."</li><li><strong>Make sure your website works on its own.</strong> Your name, phone number, services and towns should be easy to find when people search your business name. If you are not sure it does, see <a href="/guides/what-a-trade-website-needs/">what a trade website needs</a>.</li><li><strong>Keep other listings current,</strong> such as Bing Places, Apple Business Connect and any trade directories you pay for. This is when a paid platform can genuinely earn its fee.</li><li><strong>Keep asking for reviews</strong> to be ready for when the profile returns. A suspension hides your reviews along with the profile rather than deleting them, and they normally return when the profile is reinstated.</li></ul>

<h2 id="avoid-it">How do you stop it happening again?</h2><p>Once you are back, act as if you are on probation for a few weeks. Make no big changes. After that, follow these habits:</p><ul><li>Use your real business name, exactly as registered and shown on your van and invoices.</li><li>Hide your address if customers do not visit you.</li><li>Keep one profile per real location, under an owner account you control.</li><li>Set a realistic service area.</li><li>Make one change at a time, and check your profile after any listing tool or agency touches it.</li><li>Keep a folder with your registration, licence and a recent bill so a future appeal takes ten minutes.</li></ul><p>Our <a href="/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/">Google Business Profile checklist</a> walks through every field, and <a href="/guides/local-seo-for-trades/">local SEO for trades</a> explains how the profile works with your website. If you would rather have someone set it up properly, see our <a href="/services/google-business-profile/">Google Business Profile service</a>.</p>""",
 checklist=["Do not create a new profile for the same business", "Open the appeals tool and read the reason and linked policy", "Fix the name, address, service area or duplicate profile before appealing", "Gather registration, licence, tax or utility documents with a matching name and address", "Write a short, polite appeal that says what you fixed", "Attach all evidence and submit one appeal only", "Tell past customers how to reach you while the profile is down", "Keep a document folder ready and make one profile change at a time"],
 faq=[("How long does a Google Business Profile suspension last?", "It lasts until a successful appeal. Google says appeal reviews can take up to 5 business days, though owners report waits from a few days to several weeks."),
      ("Can I just create a new Google Business Profile?", "No. Google says not to create a new profile for the same business. Duplicates break the guidelines and can make the original suspension harder to lift."),
      ("Why was my Google Business Profile suspended for no reason?", "There is always a flagged policy, and the appeals tool shows it. For trades it is usually the business name, a visible home address, an ineligible address, a duplicate profile or a recent burst of edits."),
      ("Do I lose my reviews if my profile is suspended?", "Reviews are hidden while the profile is suspended because the public cannot see the profile. They are hidden, not deleted, and normally return when Google reinstates the profile."),
      ("What if my appeal is denied?", "Google offers a form to request an additional review of a denied request, where you can add evidence you did not include the first time. Fix any remaining issues before you use it."),
      ("Can I use my home address for a Google Business Profile?", "Yes, if you are a service area business working from home. Hide the address from customers on the profile, and use documents with that address as evidence if you appeal.")],
 links=["/guides/google-business-profile-checklist/", "/guides/local-seo-for-trades/", "how-to-get-google-reviews", "/guides/what-a-trade-website-needs/"],
)
